  "id": "Compound::DB00582 - Gene::5742",
  "verbalization": "Voriconazole is implicated in the pathogenesis of Pseudoporphyria, a condition that is caused by Nabumetone or Naproxen, both of which bind to Ptgs1. The evidence suggests that Voriconazole may exert a causal effect on Pseudoporphyria, which in turn leads to the activation of Ptgs1 through the binding of Nabumetone or Naproxen. The lowest common ancestor of these entities is the Oxygen Molecular Entity, indicating a shared biological pathway. The integration of these pathways highlights a potential mechanism where Voriconazole indirectly influences Ptgs1 via the induction of Pseudoporphyria, which is mediated by the action of Nabumetone or Naproxen. This relationship underscores a complex interplay between drug-induced conditions and their downstream effects on inflammatory pathways.",
